 {:seon.task/name "a-search-costs-what-it-ranks"
  :seon.task/plan "What exists, measured 2026-11-10 on main's program (a lab root, seon.cost/measure, warm; query \"a turn's diff is rendered from the page parts at two bases, never stored\", :kinds #{:var :section :schema}, k 10, no vector): one seon.ctx.search/search costs 8.7M ticks and 25 MB, of which seon.ctx.search/relevance 4.8M (seon.doc/terms tokenizing the 40 pool items' texts, 134k characters, 2.5M; item-text 0.4M; its own scoring 1.7M) and seon.doc/tokens 2.8M (seon.doc/named-hits for the 351 hits above the z cut, 2.0M; the FTS5 query 0.34M). A search answers k hits from a pool of k x related-near.

Since then, relevance's lexical feature reads each pool item's term counts kept beside the text index's row of its text (seon.doc/text-terms, counted the first time read, dropped by carry-index! for the texts a move wrote), the query's words alone, so its cost is flat in the texts' length (seon.doc-cost-test/a-search-costs-flat-in-its-texts-length: on the old code 0.61M ticks at 10 added words, 1.01M at 100, 5.00M at 1,000; now 0.51M at 10 and within 0.6M at all three).

The change left: tokens names every hit above the z cut (351 in the measured search, seon.doc/named-hits 2.0M) when a search shows k and fuses the first k x related-near: name the hits the fusion can reach (a text hit past rank r scores at most 1/(c+r) in the text ranking, so past the point where that and its best other ranking cannot reach the first k x related-near it is counted by kind, not named), or say why every one must be named. Measure on main's program first: what tokens costs of a search now that relevance's tokenizing is gone.

Proofs: seon.doc-cost-test/a-search-costs-nothing-for-sections-it-does-not-answer-from and a-search-costs-flat-in-its-texts-length (green); to write, red first: seon.doc-cost-test/a-search-costs-flat-in-the-text-hits-its-fusion-cannot-reach."
